{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1788589189784750883,"compiled_state":{"alias":"renowned-corked-hapuku","assignee":null,"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":3,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"17b5fc83a805ca1e509269b79d64b7e171ab8d59539606264462de893fc066ae","event_uuid":"a28a631b-9643-4907-98ab-089828b8a4e5","position":{"commit_sha":"aa48cb6135febf4752813d80da9201b4b5f890f0","position":"1788577322086963001-a28a631b-9643-4907-98ab-089828b8a4e5"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"942f638ac67deab177be91c16144ca4be6161b691f6923294e7eea705e6e130c","event_uuid":"6c697d7b-d2ac-4c85-9fa1-e4baaf246c40","position":{"commit_sha":"0408a7ba674c31e986048215989f9a3affc10414","position":"1788577340976676001-6c697d7b-d2ac-4c85-9fa1-e4baaf246c40"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"da725af06794234ca0fe1d238a057beb889d03012973d7cc67c2695e029798af","event_uuid":"c754d715-1e89-445a-b446-7a5d6aeb6962","position":{"commit_sha":"1bc8fb7457dd8be1a5b4ba1ca37d142ba01c9613","position":"1788577354979356001-c754d715-1e89-445a-b446-7a5d6aeb6962"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1788577322086963001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"6c697d7b-d2ac-4c85-9fa1-e4baaf246c40","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"aa40-cbda-ee38-481c"},{"link_uuid":"c754d715-1e89-445a-b446-7a5d6aeb6962","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"6202-e1c7-c57f-4897"}],"description":"## The finding\n\nEpic 6202's story **S1 (`aa40-cbda`, \"Isolate review-gate scratch on a dedicated EBS volume\") is\nCLOSED, but its deliverable is not in effect.** The volume exists, is attached, and is raw and\nunmounted; gate scratch is running on the ROOT filesystem, which is precisely the silent fallback\nS1 exists to prevent.\n\n## Measured on the live host, 2026-09-05 (SSM, read-only)\n\n```\nlsblk\n  nvme1n1   50G  xfs   /var/gerrit   vol06fa2e77a9dd97527\n  nvme0n1   60G        (root)        vol0270fcf13709cf472\n  nvme2n1   50G  ----   ----         vol06780b8557d1416b7   <- NO FSTYPE, NO MOUNTPOINT\n\nblkid /dev/nvme2n1            -> none            (no filesystem at all)\n/etc/fstab                    -> entries for /, /boot/efi, /var/gerrit ONLY; no gate-scratch line\ndu -sh /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch -> 9.3G       ON ROOT, actively written (mtime within 2 min)\nls -A  /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch -> rebar-gate-snapshots, reviewbot-nopn30zv,\n                                      reviewbot-t8kxn0i8, semgrep-mcp\ndf -h /                       -> 34G used of 60G (57%)\n.gate-scratch-required        -> ABSENT\n.gate-scratch-mounted         -> ABSENT\n```\n\nAWS confirms the volume is `in-use`, attached to `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` at `/dev/sdg`, and the NVMe\nserial on `nvme2n1` is exactly `vol06780b8557d1416b7`. So there is no ambiguity about which device\nit is — it simply was never formatted or mounted.\n\n## Why it happened\n\nThe volume was created by a `terraform apply` **after** the instance's first boot. `user_data.sh`\nruns under cloud-init on FIRST BOOT ONLY, so its `mount_ebs_volume \"${gate_scratch_volume_id}\"`\nstep has never executed for this volume. Nothing else on the host mounts it. This is a real gap in\nthe delivery path, not a bug in `user_data.sh`'s logic: the script is correct and simply never ran.\n\n## The monitoring worked — and is worth crediting\n\nBoth alarms are correctly in ALARM right now:\n\n- `rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted` — `gate_scratch_mounted` reads 0.\n- `rebar-gate-scratch-disk-high` — its metric `disk_used_percent` publishes NOTHING (there is no\n  mount to `df`), and because `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` it alarms rather than reading as\n  healthy. This is exactly the dead-man-correct behaviour `bff5-9163` was closed for.\n\nSo this was detectable and detected. What was missing is that S1 closed on code correctness without\na check that the volume actually entered service.\n\n## Why the marker design means this is DEGRADED, not BROKEN\n\n`user_data.sh:123-130` places two markers on deliberately different filesystems:\n`.gate-scratch-required` beside the mount point on ROOT (the declaration), `.gate-scratch-mounted`\ninside the mount point on the VOLUME (the proof). Declaration present + proof absent makes gate\nadmission REFUSE rather than quietly repopulate on root.\n\nHere **both** are absent, so admission does not refuse — gates run on root. That is the degraded\npath, and it has evidently been running for days. It is stable, which is why this is not a p1: root\nis at 57% with 26G free. But it means the isolation S1 bought is not being had, and a gate run that\nfills root now takes Gerrit down with it — the exact coupling S1 removed on paper.\n\n## Remediation is NOT a simple mount — read this before acting\n\n`/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` currently holds **9.3G of live, in-use working directories**\n(`reviewbot-*` sandboxes, gate snapshots, semgrep state). Mounting the volume over that path HIDES\nthose contents from any process that opens them afterwards, while leaving the 9.3G stranded on root.\nDoing it while a gate or review is mid-run would break that run.\n\nThe contents are genuinely scratch, so no data migration is needed — but a QUIET WINDOW is, and the\nstranded copy must be removed afterwards or the space is lost twice. Sequence:\n\n1. Confirm no gate/review is in flight (the review-bot is the main consumer).\n2. `mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme2n1` — safe, `blkid` confirms no filesystem, serial confirms the device.\n3. `mkdir -p /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` (exists), append\n   `UUID=<uuid> /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2` to `/etc/fstab`, `mount -a`.\n4. Assert `mountpoint -q /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` BEFORE creating any marker.\n5. `touch /var/lib/rebar/.gate-scratch-required` and\n   `touch /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch/.gate-scratch-mounted`; `chmod 0700` the mount point.\n   Order matters: creating `required` while unmounted makes admission refuse.\n6. Reclaim the stranded 9.3G on root (mount the volume elsewhere first, or clear the directory\n   before mounting).\n7. Confirm `gate_scratch_mounted` flips to 1 and `disk_used_percent` starts publishing.\n\nDeliberately NOT executed tonight: seven agent sessions are pushing changes and the review-bot is\nactively reviewing them, so there is no quiet window, and the degraded state is stable.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The gate-scratch volume is formatted, in `/etc/fstab` by UUID, and mounted at\n      `/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch`. Proven by `mountpoint -q` and `lsblk` showing the fstype.\n- [ ] Both markers exist on their intended filesystems, created in the safe order, and\n      `chmod 0700` is applied. Proven by `ls` plus confirming they are on different devices.\n- [ ] `rebar/host:gate_scratch_mounted` publishes 1 and `disk_used_percent` resumes publishing;\n      both alarms return to OK. Proven from CloudWatch, not from the host alone.\n- [ ] The stranded copy under root is reclaimed and `df /` reflects it.\n- [ ] The DELIVERY GAP is closed, not just this instance: a volume added by `terraform apply` after\n      first boot is either mounted by something that runs post-boot, or its absence is made a\n      loud, actionable signal at apply time. A fix that only mounts this one volume by hand leaves\n      the next one to be discovered the same way — by an alarm, days later.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[["relates_to","6202-e1c7-c57f-4897"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Epic 6202 S1 closed but its deliverable never entered service: the gate-scratch EBS volume is attached, RAW and unmounted, and gate scratch has been running on the root filesystem","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["a28a631b-9643-4907-98ab-089828b8a4e5","6c697d7b-d2ac-4c85-9fa1-e4baaf246c40","c754d715-1e89-445a-b446-7a5d6aeb6962"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788589189784750883,"uuid":"0c2bcbe0-3835-4fbd-bba7-5cfa0f73894d"}